# Iteration commits (allowed)

Agents **may** run git **locally** to record work during an iteration when the user has enabled this policy (default for this repo).

## Allowed

- `git status`, `git diff`, `git log` — inspect before every commit
- `git add` + `git commit` — **small, logical units** (see granularity below)
- One commit per coherent step that a reviewer could replay

## Still taboo (human unless explicitly asked)

- `git push`, `pull`, `fetch`, `merge`, `rebase`, `checkout` (branch switches), force-push, tags, releases
- `gh pr …`, opening/updating/merging PRs
- Amending commits unless user rule conditions for amend are all met
- `git config` changes

## Granularity — generous commit count

Prefer **many small commits** over one bulk commit. Audit/accounting mindset: each commit should answer “what single thing changed?”

| Good (separate commits) | Bad (one blob) |
| --- | --- |
| Program hub README, then tasks.yaml, then findings register | Entire `redesign/` in one commit |
| One STAB task’s code + its test | Unrelated package fix + redesign docs + skill trim |
| Cursor rule add, then rule that amends no-git exception | “WIP stabilization” mixing 10 concerns |

**Split when:** different scope (`redesign` vs `packages/ai/ai-knowledge` vs `.cursor/rules`), different finding/task ID, or you would need “and also” in the subject line.

**Same commit OK when:** generated artifacts from one command (e.g. `knowledge:sync` catalog + skill mirror in one pass), or test + minimal fix for that test.

## Commit message

Follow `.cursor/rules/commit-messages.mdc` exactly:

```text
<type>(<scope>): <message>
```

Examples for this program:

```text
chore(redesign): add macro expectations and migration register
chore(redesign): verify phase 0 drizzle and memory inventories
test(ai-knowledge): assert loadPlan includes recipe canonicalSkills
chore(ci): add check-skill-sources script for STAB-AI-1
chore(rules): allow granular iteration commits
```

Read `git log -15 --oneline` and match voice before committing.

## Before each commit

1. Run relevant checks for that slice (e.g. package test, `pnpm knowledge:check` if skills/recipes touched).
2. Do not commit secrets (`.env`, tokens).
3. Do not commit broken intermediate state unless the user asked for a checkpoint — prefer commit after green checks for that slice.

## Releases (batch)

Many commits on a branch ≠ many npm releases. Use **one file per package** in `.changeset/` (repo policy); version bumps ship on the **Version Packages** PR to `main`, not after each commit. Extend existing stabilization changesets instead of adding a new changeset every iteration unless a new package’s behavior changed.

## Relationship to stabilization program

When working under `redesign/`, reference task IDs in commit subjects or body only if it helps audit (`STAB-AI-2` in scope is fine). Update `redesign/tasks.yaml` state in a **dedicated** commit when verification completes, not mixed with unrelated code.
